WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.844
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:07.670
COLLEEN SHOGAN: Good morning.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.490
My name is Colleen Shogan.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.680
I work at the
Library of Congress.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.160
I'm the assistant deputy
librarian for Collections

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.090
and Services, and I'm pleased
to be here this morning

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.990
to talk to you about
some exciting new work

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.300
that we're working on here
at the Library of Congress

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.360
in Washington, DC.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.540
And in particular, I'm here to
talk to you about the National

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.710
Library Service for the Blind
and Physically Handicapped,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.900
which is a program and division
within the Library of Congress.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.400
And what I'll refer to--

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.170
this is a very long
name for a program,

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.180
but I'll refer to it going
forward as simply NLS.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.820
And that's what we
refer to it as well.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.730
I'm here to give
you an overview.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:59.470
By the way, I'm a
political scientist.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.520
I'm not a technologist
or an engineer.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.540
But I'm here to give
you an overview of NLS

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.440
and this unique program
within the federal government.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.320
As this slide says, NLS has
been in existence since 1931.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.340
And the vision of NLS,
the vision statement

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.190
is "so that all may read."

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.060
What NLS is is a free
program for those

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.400
who are visually impaired or
print-disabled to provide what

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.200
you would call audio books,
what we call talking books,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.970
to United States citizens
all across the country.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.880
And NLS, to talk to you
a little bit about it,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.440
there's about 100 people.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.290
A little bit over 100
people who work at NLS.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.110
Like I said, it is a
program or division

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.420
within the Library of Congress.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.930
It's located in the Petworth
neighborhood of Washington, DC.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.710
So a little bit apart from where
the main Library of Congress

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.770
campus is situated
on Capitol Hill.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.880
It has its own appropriation.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.330
It has its own funding
within the federal government

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.560
part of the Library of Congress,
but a separate appropriation

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.950
that's dedicated
specifically for books

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.470
for the blind and
print-disabled.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.760
And currently right now, NLS
receives about $53 million

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.530
a year in which to do this.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.030
Right now, NLS-- and this is
what I'm going to talk a little

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:31.930
bit about--

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.590
the distribution of those
audio books or talking books

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.960
is done both through an analog--

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.920
so physical delivery through the
United States Postal Service--

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.170
and also through
digital delivery.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.060
And that's how the
program works right now.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.375
Here's a few
statistics about NLS.

00:02:51.375 --> 00:02:53.250
I'll talk a little bit,
give you a little bit

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.510
about the overview.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.780
And then I want to talk to
you about how NLS works.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.270
This is a little misleading.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.730
It says that there's
970-some thousand accounts.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:08.220
That really translates
into about 450,000 users

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.590
across the United States.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.620
And in addition to
these users, there

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.360
are about 100 network
libraries that partner

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.640
with the Library of
Congress in which

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.070
to distribute these books.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.580
And this is how it works.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.230
So if you sign up for the
NLS and you are eligible,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.950
once you are determined that you
are eligible for the program,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.060
you receive an account.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.590
You also receive what we
call a digital talking books

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.940
machine in which you
can play analog delivery

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.290
of these talking books, which is
essentially a thumb drive that

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.670
is sent to your home.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.330
You could also bring
your own device

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.920
and download the books digitally
through our online website,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.720
which is called BARD.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.900
So those are the two ways that
you're able to get your books.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.170
Right now, either way,
whether you receive your books

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.030
digitally or whether
you receive them

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.900
in the mail to play them on your
digital talking books machine,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.630
most of our users rely upon the
librarians at those 100 network

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.480
libraries for
assistance in deciding

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.910
what books they
want to listen to

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.950
and curating the
list of books that

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.230
are in their queue to be read.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.600
Now I'd like to talk a little
bit about where we're headed.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.100
So that's where
we are right now.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.470
It's a very successful program.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.320
It's a very popular
program with, obviously,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.200
our users, the visually
impaired community,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.690
and also with Congress.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.380
But this is where we're headed.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.630
We are embarking into
a very exciting time

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:50.020
I think in NLS' history, the
Library of Congress' history.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.200
We're undertaking a
modernization effort

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.940
for the next five
to seven years.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.240
And the goal of this
modernization effort

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.620
is to move NLS from a
predominantly analog service--

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.760
right now about 90% of our
users receive their books

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.460
in the mail from the
US Postal Service--

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.980
to a predominantly
digital service.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.000
So that's where
we are right now,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.330
and that's where
we want to head.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.580
There are some
challenges with that.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.440
Because of course,
any changes we

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.680
want to make in the delivery
of our talking books

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.680
has to take into
account our users.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.190
And as you might imagine with
a visually impaired community

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:35.290
of users, most of our users skew
towards the older age spectrum.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.490
So we're very sensitive
about creating and taking

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.120
advantage of technologies
that will be acceptable

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.320
and can be used by our
patrons and the people who

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.900
rely upon us for books.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.720
We do have a aggressive strategy
in the next five to seven

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:53.220
years.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:57.300
As I said before, we have
currently about 450,000 users

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.800
across the United States.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.310
We would like to increase
that to 1 million users

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.580
across the United States in
the next five to seven years

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:10.130
as the result of modernization.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.690
And really what
we're talking about

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.880
is elevating
digital experiences.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.220
And what that means
to myself and how

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:22.280
I think about that is that
my vision is for NLS users

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.220
to be able to
consume talking books

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.550
and read books the same
way that you or I are

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.680
used to consuming books.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.800
And that means being able
to download and have access

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.090
to books on demand.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:40.550
If you hear about a
book on TV or the radio

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.440
or you hear about
it from a friend,

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.570
you're able to
download that book.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.760
If you read digitally,
you're able to download

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.150
that book immediately
and then begin reading it

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.630
if you so choose.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.010
If you don't like that book
or it's not what you expected,

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:58.460
you're able to switch streams
and download a different book

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.890
immediately and start that book.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.670
And having the ability to be
able to do that for myself

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:09.350
as a reader has really changed
the way I consume books.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.850
And it makes for a much
more enriching experience.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:15.870
And that's the vision that we
want to bring to our NLS users

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:16.370
right now.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.670
Not that they necessarily have
to wait three to five days

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:23.370
for that cartridge
to come in the mail,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.180
but that they're able to have
access to those audio books

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.920
right now online.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.890
And where we're headed
is relying primarily

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:34.070
on a bring-your-own-device
model in the future,

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:37.610
with perhaps a digital talking
book machine that's provided

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.800
to those users who need it.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.100
And we definitely want to
move to a technology that's

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:46.490
platform-agnostic so that
we can have people that

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.190
bring the devices that
they're comfortable with be

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:53.670
able to use them and take
advantage of NLS services.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.730
And what we're doing
right now is NLS

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:57.230
is in the very early stages--

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.590
we're really in year 0 of
this modernization effort,

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.420
with it kicking off next year.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:06.080
And what we're doing right now
is doing small pilot projects

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.820
to figure out which technologies
can best serve our user

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.400
and patron base in the future.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.320
And that brings
us to the subject

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:20.420
of today's presentation, which
is focused on the voice user

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.330
interface, which is one
of the pilot projects

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:26.870
that NLS is engaged
in this year.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.260
So trying to figure out
how can we use voice user

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:34.400
interface in the
future for our patrons

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.690
who want to search for a book.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.200
For example, I want
to read biographies.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.539
I want to read romances.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:41.990
I want to read westerns.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:45.620
How can they have that
interactive experience working

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.550
with artificial intelligence,
with a voice user interface,

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:53.090
that will enable them to get
the right books in their queue

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.910
and that they will
be able to enjoy?

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.160
They also need to be able
to listen to those books--

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.300
most of our patrons will listen
to a book from start to finish.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:05.630
For example, how you might
read a fiction book, or even

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.220
most nonfiction books.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.460
But other users will want
particular books in which they

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.950
need to skip around,
so you have to be

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.250
able to use the voice
automated interface to be

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:18.260
able to move to different
points in the book.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:21.800
For example, if you are
reading a recipe or a cookbook,

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.570
or if you're reading
a travel book,

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.400
are good examples
where you wouldn't

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.830
read that book
necessarily sequentially,

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.830
but focusing on particular
parts of the book

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.560
that you find
interesting or relevant.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.410
So right now where
the library is--

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.340
and we're going to see a demo
here from some of my colleagues

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.020
that I think is very promising.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:43.950
Right now where the
Library of Congress is,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.320
we've issued an RFI--

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.730
Request For Information-- about
search, discovery, and playback

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.640
for audiobooks for
the visually impaired

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:58.430
using smart speaker technology
on a vendor-agnostic platform.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:01.790
And that's just our process
of gathering more information

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.910
in this very early
pre-stage of modernization

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:07.550
so that we're able to make
the best strategic decisions

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.600
in the future for the
modernization of our services

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.730
to the blind, visually
impaired, and print-disabled.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.230
It's also important for us, I
will say the last thing that's

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.750
important for us, is security.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:23.390
Because the National
Library Service of the Blind

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.930
and Physically Handicapped has
an exception to legislation

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.320
to copyright
provisions that enable

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.150
them to share books to the
users of the NLS community.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.740
But we want to make
sure that whenever

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.110
we put these books
out there that only

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:42.950
our NLS users are being
authorized to listen and take

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:44.280
advantage of them.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.080
So security is also of
utmost importance for us,

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.810
and also to our patrons.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.060
So now I'm going to turn it
over to my colleagues who

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.230
are going to talk a little
bit about their work,

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.700
their technical work in this
area that they've done already.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.320
And they will also
show us a demo

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:03.710
which is really a product
of all their hard work.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.952
PRABHU PALANISAMY:
Thanks, Colleen.

00:11:07.952 --> 00:11:08.910
Good morning, everyone.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.030
My name is Prabhu Palanisamy.

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:13.100
I'm from the SpringML team.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.670
We had the privilege of working
with the Library of Congress

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:22.670
in building the pilot and
demo associated with it.

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:27.740
Show of hands-- I'll pretend
that I will be able to see--

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:33.070
how many of you in the
room has a smartphone?

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:37.780
How many of you have a Google
Home or a device in your house?

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:39.940
How many of you have
more than one device?

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.190
That's the age we
live in, right?

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:50.060
Obviously, parents
in the room know

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:54.230
how their kids are able to
make use of the Google Homes

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.720
effectively than us.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.960
Voice interface is
becoming their primary

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.960
more of communication for
many of the applications that

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:04.820
are being built today.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:09.800
Here is an image of
the talking devices

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.500
that are being developed.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:15.440
And the technology
enablement happened in waves,

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.440
innovation-wise, at the
Library of Congress.

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:22.490
This was displayed in the Hall
of the Library of Congress

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.200
when we were doing the project.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:26.600
So cassette record players.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.780
And the one in the
middle is the device that

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.990
is used by the patrons today.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.760
They have to go to the library.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.520
They have to get the
right device in place

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.740
and then use it to read books.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:43.980
Like, when we were
surveying, on the project,

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:45.950
there were patrons
who read, like,

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:48.230
three to four books a week.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:52.070
And they read at a
pace of around 1.75

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:57.440
to 2x compared to a normal
reader who is reading visually.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.440
They play it fast.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.503
They are able to grasp
the content much faster

00:13:01.503 --> 00:13:02.670
than the rest of the people.

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:08.270
[? Two ?] levels
[? sets, ?] right?

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.410
From a technology project,
this is very, very easy

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.670
for us to map it.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:15.810
That's the device.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:19.190
That's the technology that
they are exposed today.

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.650
And the solution is right
in front of you guys.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:26.180
Having a device that they
can interact over voice

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:29.740
and get the content that
they are looking for.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:34.420
To look at it, for a general
audience, we have e-books.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.270
That's the primary choice
that we use these days.

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:40.110
Apps, Google
Assistant, mobile apps,

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.200
smart phones that
we interact with.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:45.770
And again, the third one is
the conversational interface.

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.030
That's the exposure
of technology

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.370
for common people like us.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:57.010
And how about having this
technology to the other folks

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:59.470
that we want to enable them?

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:02.980
Like Colleen mentioned,
they have a vision

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.000
of making sure that
the 400,000 folks are

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.490
getting enabled and getting
the value out of it.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.670
In simple terms,
this is the vision

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.640
we wanted to implement
on a project.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:25.390
Sitting in a home on
evening when they come back,

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:28.340
when they say play a
book, and the system

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:31.190
starts to be able to
understand the user profile.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:36.530
Goes through the security, the
authentication, the look up,

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:39.110
and then the book
starts to play.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:40.430
Simple terms.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.800
But imagine for a
moment how powerful

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:48.370
the life changes for folks
who can get this value.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:51.840
And then from the
Library of Congress team,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.050
how about we do it
for all the books?

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:56.430
The journals?

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.245
Publications?

00:14:57.245 --> 00:14:59.370
Way [? there ?] [? are a ?]
[? search ?] [? that ?]

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.520
[? the ?] [? team ?] [? has ?]
[? it, ?] and we can enable it

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.720
for the folks for
a general audience.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:10.670
Now, I think we
have set the stage.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.560
This is the device
that the patrons use.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.140
And this is the end goal.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:19.490
You know, using a conversational
interface and play the books.

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:23.340
How did we do it, and what are
some of the success criterias

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:27.260
to consider for a
project like this?

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:30.780
Number one, it's
a conversational.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:34.020
It's not like a resource--
you click on a button

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:40.400
or open a page, and then go
through a normal application.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.930
It should be a
human conversation.

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.440
Hey, I'm interested in
a science fiction book,

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:47.900
or I'm interested
in a cooking recipe.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:52.630
Can you list out the books
that are of interest to me?

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:56.350
I'm interested in a book by
a specific author or a genre

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:57.950
that I need to look up.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:00.460
It needs to be a
highly conversational

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.498
in terms of interaction-wise.

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:10.470
The other thing is interactive,
where it's not about a book

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.910
and the system plays it.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:15.200
How do we make it
more interactive?

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:18.180
Like go to the next
chapter, pause,

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:22.125
come back to the
synopsis of the book,

00:16:22.125 --> 00:16:26.190
or read me the title
and the contents.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:27.990
We want to make it
highly interactive,

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.840
so that way it's not
about finding the resource

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.170
and playing the
resource, but how

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:38.370
would a human converse and
interact with an application?

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.570
The last and very
important one is scale.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.930
This is not a project for
a certain subset of people

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:46.880
or certain subset of books.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.190
We want to solve it at scale.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.860
When we want to
load 100 books, it

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:55.340
should be the same process
to load 1,000, 10,000,

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.580
and a million books.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:01.460
Imagine for a moment
we solve the problems,

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:06.260
and then in order to scale it,
probably enterprise consultants

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:08.060
or IT in the room
would have gone

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.849
through the struggle of
building a project is one thing.

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:12.260
Building an application.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:15.890
What's the scale in
that application?

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:19.310
We want to do it in one
platform that supports

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:20.926
this end-to-end pipeline.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.079
So without further
any delay, I would

