00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:39.280
OK.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:41.480
So let me start
with what challenges

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:43.020
we faced with this project.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:46.250
So when we started
the project, we

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:49.050
were told about the
scale of the project--

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:54.080
how many users were there,
what sort of information we had

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:58.520
about how many books were
there, and the size of the book.

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:02.220
For example, if you want to
play some of these audio files,

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:03.290
they're really large.

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:05.380
I mean, they could be
running in GBs, right?

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:08.930
They are really large books.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:11.000
We had to get the
information instantly.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.083
There are millions of books
that should be stored.

00:23:13.083 --> 00:23:16.620
It should have various
categories of information,

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:17.420
like the title.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:20.110
You've got various different
synonyms possibily.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.550
You've got author names.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.750
And then, like
Colleen mentioned,

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:28.852
security is a big deal.

00:23:28.852 --> 00:23:31.310
We had to make sure that the
audio files that we are using,

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:33.500
they have to be secure.

00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:36.530
And they are not
accessible by anybody

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:39.200
else other than the
patrons who are already

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:45.110
registered with the system.

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:48.440
And obviously, we have
to target massive scales

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.800
so that if the users
go beyond millions,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:53.180
we have to take care of that.

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:56.900
And as the number
of books gets added,

00:23:56.900 --> 00:23:59.480
we have the system
in place which

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:04.700
allows the administrators
to seamlessly add

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:07.580
those books into the system.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.920
Make it available.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:11.690
So all interesting challenges.

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:14.720
And more importantly,
with Dialogflow,

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:18.310
make it extremely
conversational and intelligent.

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:20.060
That's the key here.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:23.150
So that if someone even says the
wrong things, if the books are

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:27.320
not there, make sure
you propose new ways

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:30.410
of searches or different
intelligent responses.

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:31.280
That's the key part.

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:39.750
So I'll go with the high
level architecture over here.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:43.200
So if you see, there
are five boxes.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.290
The first two are
the channels which

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:49.100
are available for
accessing the system.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:51.020
You've got support
from Google Assistant

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.270
on mobile and Google Home.

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:55.400
You can use these two
channels, or Google Mini

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:58.730
as well, to connect to
this particular system--

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.640
the library.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:02.240
The conversation
management obviously

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:07.430
is being handled by
the Dialogflow engine.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:10.390
And finally, you
got the fulfillment,

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:12.730
and that's going to be handled
by the Cloud Functions.

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:15.850
That's what we've
used using Node.js.

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:20.670
The Cloud Functions in turn
connect to the Firestore.

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:24.240
That's where all the
metadata about these books

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:25.570
is being stored.

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:28.030
So what's the title?

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:28.860
What's the author?

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:32.010
What are the chapters there?

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:34.530
And then finally the
storage of the audio

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.115
files itself is going
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:44.690
So most of the scale
issues-- obviously, this is

00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:46.010
what Google is great at, right?

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:48.230
I mean, it provides scale.

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:50.450
So using the Google
Cloud Storage,

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:54.260
we were able to upload
terabytes of data.

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:56.480
No issues at all.

00:25:56.480 --> 00:26:00.560
And we were able to collect
metadata about the books,

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:02.420
upload it into the Firestore.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:03.990
No issues at all.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:07.010
And it's not mentioned
here, but we also

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:10.690
had to make sure that the
metadata that's getting

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:14.290
added into Firestore-- we have
the right jobs which collect

00:26:14.290 --> 00:26:19.310
that information and create
entities out of that,

00:26:19.310 --> 00:26:21.445
and get it in sync
into the Dialogflow.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.900
So that's the high
level architecture.

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.420
So let's go into the flow now.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:30.050
What exactly is happening?

00:26:30.050 --> 00:26:32.870
What are the systems
being used out here?

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:38.100
So a user speaks
into Google Home,

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.680
and the first task that
happens there is it

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:45.928
makes sure that the user who
has logged into the Google Home.

00:26:45.928 --> 00:26:47.220
You have to set up Google Home.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:50.940
And at that point, you obviously
need to sign up, right?

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:52.560
And that's the
information that we

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:56.320
are going to use through
the whole system.

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:59.360
There's SSOs-- Single Sign
On-- all over the place.

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:02.790
And the start point is
really the authentication

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:06.030
into the Google Home.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:06.990
So what happens next?

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:11.140
So a user speaks
into Google Home.

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:14.200
And then that request
goes into the Actions

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.970
on Google, which is the
platform that Google provides

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:25.800
for executing your software
or actions on the Google

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:28.200
Home or the Google Assistant.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:32.880
At this point, the
voice recording

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:37.780
that was done through Google
Home, is converted into text.

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:40.990
And then it goes
into the Dialogflow.

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:44.760
When the sentence, the converted
sentence reaches Dialogflow,

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:48.500
it's essentially going
to take that sentence,

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.855
convert it into the intents, and
extract the entities required.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:28:01.020
Once you have the
intent in place,

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:04.380
we are connecting this
system to the Cloud Function.

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:07.440
So this information is
part of the Cloud Function.

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:10.450
And that's where we have
intent handlers in place,

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:13.590
which are going to
read this information

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:17.050
and do the relevant tasks.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:22.080
So what is it that a particular
intent is required to do?

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.810
So let's go to the next slide.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:27.460
So suppose we had
to do book search.

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:30.212
And when you have
to do book search,

00:28:30.212 --> 00:28:31.670
someone is going
to say, all right,

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:34.210
I want to get a book
based on a certain genre.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:36.610
So the Cloud Function
is going to receive

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:41.200
that particular intent,
which is to search for a book

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:42.640
by a genre.

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:47.890
And it is going to the
Firestore and making

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:51.380
sure we have those
kind of books.

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:55.270
And if it does, then it returns
that particular response,

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.640
sends it back to the
Dialogflow in text,

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:00.430
which is then sent back
to the Actions on Google.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:03.970
And Actions on Google is going
to convert that text into voice

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:07.460
and release it back
to Google Home.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:11.230
So that's the flow for
the typical searches.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.450
And when you want
to screen the audio,

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:18.670
the same thing happens all over.

00:29:18.670 --> 00:29:23.110
The request is going to the
Google Home, then converted

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:24.532
to the Actions on Google.

00:29:24.532 --> 00:29:25.990
The voice is
converted to the text.

00:29:25.990 --> 00:29:27.370
Goes to Dialogflow.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:28.900
Cloud Function.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:30.550
And in the Cloud
Function, we now

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:33.520
know that the intent
is to play an audio.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.140
And when you want
to play an audio,

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:39.040
then you first want to check
if that particular audio file

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:40.070
exists or not.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:42.770
So we go to the Firestore,
get the information

00:29:42.770 --> 00:29:45.560
of whether the
audio exists or not.

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:48.520
And once we find that
the audio exists,

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:51.760
we have systems in place
where we have a mapping

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:56.270
essentially that the file
which exists in the Firestore,

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:58.870
we know that there is a
certain file in Cloud Storage.

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:03.310
We go and essentially hit
the Cloud Storage and check

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:05.170
for that file.

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:08.690
That file is being read back and
sent to the Actions on Google.

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:11.800
And that is what is
starting to pretty much play

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:13.610
the whole audio file.

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:15.860
And once it starts, it
takes over from there.

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:22.970
And it's playing-- it's
allowing you to pause,

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:26.220
fast forward,
repeat, start over,

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:29.710
and all the good functionalities
that Actions on Google

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:31.450
provides.

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:35.200
So for those who want to
know of the technical detail

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:40.890
here, like I said, there are
huge files involved over here--

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:41.830
GBs.

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:44.530
So one of the big challenge
that we had was, all right.

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:46.340
How do we read this out?

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:50.437
If you're going to read up GBs
of data and put it into memory,

00:30:50.437 --> 00:30:51.770
you know what's going to happen.

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:53.680
It's going to crash
the system, right?

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:55.840
So we had to make
sure it's streaming.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:59.410
So we use the media object that
Actions on Google provides.

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:02.520
And luckily, that handles
streaming brilliantly.

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:07.990
We were able to just go
forward to, like, an hour

00:31:07.990 --> 00:31:11.290
into the book, and it
just continues playing.

00:31:11.290 --> 00:31:12.550
No issues at all.

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:14.380
Amazing stuff there, right?

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:15.520
We were able to go back.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:16.930
Ask it to go back in time.

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:17.650
Start over.

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:19.010
No problems at all.

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:21.130
So the streaming was a big deal.

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:24.190
And luckily, the media
object that Actions on Google

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:27.650
provides handles
that beautifully.

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:31.340
And like I said, the security
is another interesting aspect.

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:35.120
And I have not really
mentioned that explicitly

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:37.680
in the slide over there.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:40.650
But when you hit
the Cloud Function,

00:31:40.650 --> 00:31:43.690
there's a second level of
security happening over there.

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:46.810
So first level is where
the Google Home itself

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:49.352
is saying that, hey, I've
authenticated myself.

00:31:49.352 --> 00:31:50.560
It goes to Actions on Google.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.330
It's authenticated there.

00:31:52.330 --> 00:31:54.700
And then the SSO passes on.

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:58.010
And then Dialogflow says hey,
we're all good with this user.

00:31:58.010 --> 00:32:00.310
But when it hits the
Cloud Function, what we do

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:03.510
is essentially take
those credentials back--

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:05.110
especially the email ID.

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:10.000
And then in the back
end, we have the patrons

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.050
basically registered.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:13.210
So there is a
separate system going

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:16.810
to be in place which is
registering these users.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:18.270
And the Cloud Function is--

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:20.020
obviously, that's
stored in the Firestore.

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:24.375
And we are making sure that
those users are authenticated

00:32:24.375 --> 00:32:26.170
a second time.

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:29.870
And only those users
can access the files.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:32.415
And again, the important
thing is since it's copyright,

00:32:32.415 --> 00:32:33.790
it's stored in
the cloud storage.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:35.888
But we need to make
sure that it's not

00:32:35.888 --> 00:32:37.180
possible to download the files.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:38.810
So it's all being streamed.

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:44.600
So there's no chance of
these files being downloaded.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:47.390
So those are the details
that I wanted to present--

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:49.700
the demo, the
architecture, and the flow.

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:51.640
With that, I'll
hand over to Dan,

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:54.770
who is going to cover what's
the art of the possible

00:32:54.770 --> 00:32:57.238
with Dialogflow.

00:32:57.238 --> 00:32:58.280
DAN ORBACH: Thanks, Amit.

00:33:01.260 --> 00:33:03.228
Good morning, everybody.

00:33:03.228 --> 00:33:04.770
So far what we've
heard-- we've heard

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.140
from Colleen about the
National Library Service

00:33:07.140 --> 00:33:09.150
and its mission
to enable reading

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:12.210
for the blind and
physically handicapped.

00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:16.310
We've heard from Prabhu and Amit
about the pilot that we ran,

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:19.080
the various architectures
for that pilot,

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:20.790
the challenges for that pilot.

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:25.095
What I'm going to talk
about is going to be the--

00:33:25.095 --> 00:33:26.470
what I'm going to
talk about will

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:29.080
be kind of taking this into
the art of the possible.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:32.050
What can we do that we
haven't done in the pilot

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:34.270
to extend it and
add features that

00:33:34.270 --> 00:33:36.360
will be useful for the patrons?

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.340
I will go through some key
features in Dialogflow,

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:43.090
as well as other use cases that
are being used with Dialogflow.

00:33:43.090 --> 00:33:45.190
And we'll also go through
the speech-to-text

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:48.340
and the text-to-Speech
APIs briefly, again

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:51.550
with highlight of key features
and highlight of case studies

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.670
that are being used today.

00:33:54.670 --> 00:33:57.160
So we saw a cool demo.

00:33:57.160 --> 00:34:00.850
We saw Amit try
different things.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:01.890
Actually change up.

00:34:01.890 --> 00:34:05.530
You heard all the ums
and ahs, and thinking

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:08.830
about how to ask the question--

00:34:08.830 --> 00:34:11.480
all the things that are
inherent in human speech.

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:13.179
And when you think
about it, when

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:16.840
we're saying we want the
interface to be conversational,

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:17.940
what we're saying is--

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:19.420
I can speak in the affirmative.

00:34:19.420 --> 00:34:21.580
I can answer a
question with yes,

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:24.250
or I can answer it with
absolutely, or sure,

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:27.570
or definitely, or let's
go ahead with that.

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:31.252
And a formal interactive
voice response system,

00:34:31.252 --> 00:34:33.460
it's going to be very, very
difficult for that system

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:36.050
to understand what it is
that I'm trying to say.

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:37.929
And therefore you
need some system

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:41.380
that understands the nuance
and syntax of the language

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:44.350
to be able to translate that
into something very specific

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:47.540
and get around that my
intent was to move forward.

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.500
My answer was an affirmative.

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:56.719
So when we now look, how
effective was that demo,

00:34:56.719 --> 00:35:01.413
and can the patrons use that
effectively, the first thing

00:35:01.413 --> 00:35:03.080
we want to look at
is actually something

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:04.080
that's called Analytics.

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:08.720
It's actually available
already today in Dialogflow.

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:13.360
And when we scale the
system to many, many users,

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:15.020
short of just
serving the users--

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:17.840
which we certainly want to do
to understand their experience

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:18.980
of the system--

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:21.710
wouldn't it be nice
if we could get a flow

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:23.780
tree of what are the intents?

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:25.280
And by the way,
when we say intents,

00:35:25.280 --> 00:35:26.430
we're talking about actions.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:27.620
What is it that
I'm trying to do?

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:28.703
I'm trying to play a book.

00:35:28.703 --> 00:35:30.290
I'm trying to search a book.

00:35:30.290 --> 00:35:32.360
Where do the users
actually focus?

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.100
So you're seeing
here a depiction--

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:37.130
this is direct from
Dialogflow Enterprise.

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:39.830
And the thicker lines
show you the percentage

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:44.450
or the more popular flow of that
conversation of the intents.

00:35:44.450 --> 00:35:47.240
And so we know where the
users are navigating today.

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:51.050
And that gives us a level of
insight into what is being used

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:54.020
or potentially what should
be changed or enhanced

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:54.860
in the system.

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:58.640
How many times do we fall
back into a no-match action,

00:35:58.640 --> 00:36:00.540
and what do we need
to do about that?

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:01.970
How many actions--
how deep do we

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:06.560
need to go from specifying
an intent to actually getting

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:09.410
a fulfillment or action behind
it, like a playback of a book

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:11.540
or the list of books
that we're looking for?

00:36:11.540 --> 00:36:12.860
So how deep is this?

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:18.740
And all of that is really
important in understanding

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:20.430
the effectiveness of your model.

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:25.580
Now you also noticed, or you may
have noticed, that there are--

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:29.690
so Dialogflow can support
multiple languages.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:33.080
This particular interaction
that you heard on demo

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:36.740
was actually set on its
preferences on Google Home,

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:38.690
or specifically, the Assistant.

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:41.240
So that locale and
the dialect can

00:36:41.240 --> 00:36:45.987
change to suit the
patrons' user preferences.

00:36:45.987 --> 00:36:48.070
Now there's a thing called
the conversation style.

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:49.570
And it would be
naive of me to think

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:51.640
that that interaction
that you heard

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:54.070
is actually going
to serve everybody--

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:57.220
a full set of diverse
set of patrons.

00:36:57.220 --> 00:36:59.110
And the reason is
that we all have

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:00.640
slightly different preferences.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:04.060
I mean, you saw in the demo
it said, you know, say one

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:04.720
for this book.

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:05.910
Say two for this book.

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:07.150
Say three for that book.

00:37:07.150 --> 00:37:08.950
I may or may not
like that interface.

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:10.840
I may prefer to
just say the name.

00:37:10.840 --> 00:37:12.640
I may prefer, maybe
when I learn it,

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:15.640
I may prefer to just say
one, or the first one,

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:18.490
or the third one, and not
have it read out to me

00:37:18.490 --> 00:37:20.480
that instruction.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.840
So the notion of a
conversation style

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:24.940
is something that
can be introduced.

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:26.450
The art of the possible.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:29.110
So to give you a quick example
on the one end of this,

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:32.610
I might have a very
verbose conversation.

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.748
The Assistant, the Dialogflow
app comes and says,

00:37:35.748 --> 00:37:36.790
what do you want to read?

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:38.197
I have books of this kind.

00:37:38.197 --> 00:37:39.280
I have books of that kind.

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:40.780
These are the types
of instructions

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:43.390
you can give me so I can
help you find your book.

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:47.200
When you make a selection, just
like we heard with Amit when

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:48.790
he selected "Pride
and Prejudice,"

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:50.388
the system came back
and said, you've

00:37:50.388 --> 00:37:51.680
selected "Pride and Prejudice."

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:52.900
Is that correct?

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:54.550
And he could say
yes, it is correct.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.860
So when I'm a first
time user of the system,

00:37:56.860 --> 00:38:00.010
I want to get that comfort
that the system understands me.

00:38:00.010 --> 00:38:02.050
And it knows what it is
that I'm trying to do.

00:38:02.050 --> 00:38:05.050
On the flip side, I might
be an experienced user.

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:06.433
I want to cut to the chase.

00:38:06.433 --> 00:38:07.600
Don't give me all this info.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:09.190
Let's just play this book.

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:10.150
Think of the shortcuts.

00:38:10.150 --> 00:38:12.080
Think of single utterances.

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:13.870
So how can I design
a system that,

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:17.020
based on my user preferences,
will give me the interface that

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:18.238
best suits me?

00:38:18.238 --> 00:38:19.780
And that's what we
were talking about

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:22.420
with conversational styles.

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:25.430
Now, book collections--
think of it

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:29.780
much like a wish list or
a recommendation engine.

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:32.790
So as a user, we're already
said as a patron, I will log in.

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:33.920
I will authenticate.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:35.320
That kind of proves who I am.

00:38:35.320 --> 00:38:38.630
We know that I've been
eligible for the program.

00:38:38.630 --> 00:38:40.880
We know that's important for
digital rights management

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:44.330
perspective purposes.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:46.220
And when I create a
book collection, when

00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:48.140
I say recommendation engine,
the first thing a tech

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:50.330
audience will say is, yeah,
we know what that means.

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:51.380
Absolutely.

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:53.750
But in reality, here what
you have is the patrons

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.900
have a very personal
relationship, in many cases,

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:58.550
with their local librarians.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:02.120
As Colleen mentioned, they
rely on their guidance

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:03.030
and knowledge.

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:06.000
And they appreciate that.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:07.460
But what we can do
here potentially

00:39:07.460 --> 00:39:11.330
is have these local librarians
insert books into your wish

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:15.350
list so that you still maintain
that personal interaction,

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:19.400
but move forward
with-- you can still

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:22.730
augment your system-generated
recommendations

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:28.070
or personally inserted notes.

00:39:28.070 --> 00:39:30.560
The other thing is to be
able to add annotations

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:31.590
and notes to the book.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:34.160
So for this, perhaps I'm
doing some kind of study.

00:39:34.160 --> 00:39:36.650
Think a study guide where
I can add my own notes

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:37.710
and information.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:40.340
And I want to maintain
that alongside that place

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.010
in the book so that
I can come back

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:46.490
to this later on, and perhaps
even search out later on.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:47.520
Content searching.

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:51.110
So in the pilot today,
primarily you had three things.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:54.050
You had the book title, you had
author, and you had the genre.

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:57.590
But what if I want to search
for chocolate cake recipes

00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:01.498
across a corpus of
books or content?

00:40:01.498 --> 00:40:03.290
So now being able to
search within the book

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:05.990
and have it come back and
provide me some options.

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:08.180
That's certainly
art of the possible.

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:10.400
Dynamically assembling
content would

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:11.960
be something similar
to what I just

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:14.780
mentioned, where I'm taking
content from multiple places

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:17.540
every time that
Mr. So-and-so has

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:19.410
spoken on a particular topic.

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.190
And I want to get all
of those clipped out,

00:40:22.190 --> 00:40:24.380
basically, and created
into a dynamic book.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:26.600
Think of it that way.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:28.520
Notifications, reminders.

00:40:28.520 --> 00:40:31.010
Again, that could be
new content available.

00:40:31.010 --> 00:40:34.670
That could be my book club
book is about to start reading.

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:36.680
My class is about to start.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:40.208
Just having Google
Home notify me.

00:40:40.208 --> 00:40:42.500
And then finally, real quick
on the art of the possible

00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:45.080
is just what
happens to me a lot.

00:40:45.080 --> 00:40:47.480
I start reading a book,
and then I get distracted.

00:40:47.480 --> 00:40:49.490
And two, three days
later, I'm back.

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:51.300
Or maybe two or
three weeks later,

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.570
I'm back or maybe even
a month later, I'm back.

00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:55.612
And now I've forgotten
everything that I've read.

00:40:55.612 --> 00:40:57.570
Because my memory
needs an upgrade,

00:40:57.570 --> 00:40:59.360
and I just don't
remember things anymore.

00:40:59.360 --> 00:41:01.970
So wouldn't it be nice,
similar to TV shows

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:04.500
where you see in the
last episode of this,

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:05.943
this is what happened.

00:41:05.943 --> 00:41:07.610
Wouldn't it be nice
if we could actually

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:11.060
go back a couple of paragraphs,
summarize that content--

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:13.700
programmatically--
and come back with,

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:16.080
this is what you read
last, just to remind you

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:19.960
the context of where you were?

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:22.300
And a little bit
further into the future

00:41:22.300 --> 00:41:24.050
is voice-enabled research.

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:28.460
I'll move forward in
the interest of time.

00:41:28.460 --> 00:41:31.510
So we spoke about
analytics as well already.

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:33.250
But sentiment
analysis-- so think

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:37.940
about, as the interaction
grows and moves forward,

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:41.928
what kind of information can
I get about the language that

00:41:41.928 --> 00:41:43.720
is used by the patron,
and are they getting

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:45.400
frustrated with the interface?

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:49.300
Again, a way for us
to very quickly assess

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:52.138
the effectiveness
of an interface.

00:41:52.138 --> 00:41:54.430
There are training phrases
that you use with Dialogflow

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:55.248
to train it.

00:41:55.248 --> 00:41:57.790
And that's how you can tell it
how to use different language.

00:41:57.790 --> 00:42:01.870
And very briefly, the
training phrase will actually

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:03.920
be taken by Dialogflow
and it'll match it

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:06.340
to an intent or an action
you're trying to achieve,

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:08.050
and will match
entities or things

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:10.450
that you're talking about
within your conversation.

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:13.180
It's a way for you
to train something

00:42:13.180 --> 00:42:16.960
that is not so formal in terms
of the conversational aspect.

00:42:20.110 --> 00:42:22.470
I'll move forward past
some of the items here.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.020
Maybe touch on
multi-platform real quick.

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:28.390
There are a variety
of integrations.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:30.060
So for example, I
could just as well

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:34.920
have used Alexa, Cortana, Slack,
Skype, Twitter, as integrations

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:35.910
into Dialogflow.

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:39.570
And the benefit there is
having the same engine

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:41.640
behind the scenes, but
different interfaces

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:44.130
from the patrons' perspective.

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:47.490
And real quick, on
two of the use cases--

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:49.470
you're seeing a bunch
of use cases here.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:51.150
I'll touch on two real quick.

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:54.570
One of them is Starkey,
Starkey Hearing Technologies.

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:56.310
It's a company that
makes hearing aids.

00:42:56.310 --> 00:43:01.590
And they have I guess a brand
of Livio and Livio AI hearing

00:43:01.590 --> 00:43:02.400
aids.

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:05.820
And they're actually the world's
first "healthable" hearing aid

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:07.890
with embedded sensors and AI.

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:11.040
And what they do is they make
it easy for their customers who

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:14.310
use their hearing aids to
understand what features,

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:16.860
what settings they can
use on their hearing aids.

00:43:16.860 --> 00:43:18.870
And they actually
use Dialogflow to be

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:22.228
able to enable that setting
so they can talk to their app.

00:43:22.228 --> 00:43:23.520
They have an app called Thrive.

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:27.200
And they can talk to this
app and actually enhance

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:31.260
the settings that makes sense
for them through Dialogflow.

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:35.940
A second example is a company
called Polydice out of Taiwan.

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:38.790
And what they did was they
built a social network

00:43:38.790 --> 00:43:42.870
to enable users to be able
to find and share recipes--

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:43.710
literally.

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:46.672
So find and share recipes.

00:43:46.672 --> 00:43:47.880
And they're using Dialogflow.

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:49.367
Why is that interesting?

00:43:49.367 --> 00:43:50.950
Well number one is
you can talk to it.

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:52.825
And you tell it this is
what I'm looking for.

00:43:52.825 --> 00:43:54.300
These are my preferences.

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.950
But secondarily,
I'm taking those,

00:43:55.950 --> 00:43:58.533
capturing those preferences, and
I can create a recommendation

00:43:58.533 --> 00:44:00.240
engine behind it
for what recipes

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:04.080
I might want to give you, or
which users I might suggest

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:05.890
you share your recipe with.

00:44:05.890 --> 00:44:08.690
So that's real interesting.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:13.580
So Speech API.

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:16.310
Speech API is actually broken
into two pieces speech-to-text

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:17.605
and text-to-speech.

00:44:17.605 --> 00:44:19.730
Most of these examples that
I'm going to talk about

00:44:19.730 --> 00:44:22.610
are speech-to-text, but I
will touch on text-to-speech

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:23.690
as well.

00:44:23.690 --> 00:44:28.923
The ability to handle a single
or multi-channels, which

00:44:28.923 --> 00:44:31.340
basically means that if I have
multiple speakers, each one

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:34.930
on a different channel, that
I'd be able to take that all

00:44:34.930 --> 00:44:40.160
and incorporate it into a single
transcription of what's said.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:42.990
The ability to
detect the language.

00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:45.740
So I can actually provided
with a list of four languages

00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:48.830
that I'm expecting and
have it detect the language

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:52.540
and transcribe on the fly.

00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:54.920
The ability to use voice models.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:56.140
This is very interesting.

00:44:56.140 --> 00:44:58.170
So you have the
ability-- you know,

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:01.690
is the source of the
audio a phone call?

00:45:01.690 --> 00:45:04.330
Is the source of
the audio a command?

00:45:04.330 --> 00:45:06.735
Is the source of
the audio a video?

00:45:06.735 --> 00:45:08.860
And when you think about
it-- so if it's a command,

00:45:08.860 --> 00:45:12.220
it's typically almost a
single utterance or something.

00:45:12.220 --> 00:45:16.565
It will be a
2-to-10-second invocation.

00:45:16.565 --> 00:45:18.190
If it's a phone call,
it might be, say,

00:45:18.190 --> 00:45:20.030
a 4 minute conversation.

00:45:20.030 --> 00:45:23.830
And if it's a video, it
could be 2 hours-plus.

00:45:23.830 --> 00:45:25.390
And you might say, damn.

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:27.080
Four minutes on the phone.

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:27.795
There's no way.

00:45:27.795 --> 00:45:29.170
And I might be
aging myself here,

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:33.160
because I remember phone calls
going into hours and hours,

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.080
way eclipsing the video.

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:37.930
Having said that, the
youngsters of today

00:45:37.930 --> 00:45:40.550
are loath to speak on the
phone in the first place,

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:43.010
so I would say it
averages out nicely.

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:45.970
But seriously, the artifact
that we're looking for--

00:45:45.970 --> 00:45:49.420
each of those environments
brings a different collection

00:45:49.420 --> 00:45:51.500
of artifacts with it.

00:45:51.500 --> 00:45:55.210
For example, the command
is a single person.

00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:57.110
And they're usually
short utterances.

00:45:57.110 --> 00:45:59.200
The phone call
will be two people

00:45:59.200 --> 00:46:00.463
with a static on the line.

00:46:00.463 --> 00:46:01.630
There might be other things.

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:04.060
Perhaps two to three people,
depending on the conference.

00:46:04.060 --> 00:46:06.760
And the video will bring, again,
different background noise

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.360
and a different context.

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:11.170
So each one of them can be
handled by different models

00:46:11.170 --> 00:46:15.130
to make it more accurate.

00:46:15.130 --> 00:46:16.840
Real quick-- phrase hints.

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:20.230
Phrase hints is a way for us
to train the dictionary so

00:46:20.230 --> 00:46:23.210
to speak, of the transcription.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:27.430
So at Next, we announced
something called Anthos.

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:30.070
Actually, "antho,"
I believe, comes

00:46:30.070 --> 00:46:31.720
from the Greek word of flower.

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:33.140
Anthos is a new word.

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:35.650
Maybe my model doesn't
know what it is.

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:38.530
Well, I can add a phrase here
to say when you hear this,

00:46:38.530 --> 00:46:41.830
don't break it out
into "an--" gibberish.

00:46:41.830 --> 00:46:43.840
I want you to
actually say Anthos.

00:46:43.840 --> 00:46:46.000
So I'm going to give it
as a phrase hint for it

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:47.772
to recognize
something that I want.

00:46:47.772 --> 00:46:48.730
And it could be a word.

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:49.688
It could be a sentence.

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:55.690
I'll just touch on two more
here real quick, because I'm

00:46:55.690 --> 00:46:57.650
running out of time.

00:46:57.650 --> 00:46:59.530
One of them is
speaker diarization.

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:01.250
So we talked about
multiple channels.

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:03.740
We might have four
people in a conversation.

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:05.290
How do we transcribe that?

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:07.930
And what we're doing
with speaker diarization

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:11.860
is we're actually providing
a speaker number or a talker

00:47:11.860 --> 00:47:13.810
number along with
the transcription.

00:47:13.810 --> 00:47:15.310
So you're seeing the graphic.

00:47:15.310 --> 00:47:18.280
You've got one speaker in
red, one speaker in yellow.

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:21.460
What I'm able to do is say,
speaker 1 said the following.

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:23.220
Speaker 2 followed up
with this response.

00:47:23.220 --> 00:47:25.570
Speaker 1 continued.

00:47:25.570 --> 00:47:28.840
And it can actually do
that for as many speakers

00:47:28.840 --> 00:47:31.390
as it can recognize
within a channel.

00:47:31.390 --> 00:47:36.170
So as long as it's not too many
and too close, it can do that.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:36.920
All right.

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:38.940
Two quick examples here.

00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:41.510
One of them is on
the speech-to-text.

00:47:41.510 --> 00:47:45.152
VTCSecure is a
technology organization

00:47:45.152 --> 00:47:46.610
that makes services
for the hearing

00:47:46.610 --> 00:47:48.950
impaired or the deaf community.

00:47:48.950 --> 00:47:50.870
And specifically,
they had an initiative

00:47:50.870 --> 00:47:52.745
where they were helping
them find jobs and go

00:47:52.745 --> 00:47:53.690
through job fairs.

00:47:53.690 --> 00:47:55.940
And they used the
speech-to-text capability

00:47:55.940 --> 00:47:58.550
to help the hearing
impaired actually converse

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:00.970
with their interviewer.

00:48:00.970 --> 00:48:03.540
A second example would be
on the text-to-speech side

00:48:03.540 --> 00:48:04.500
of the house.

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:06.850
You may be familiar
with Ingram Content.

00:48:06.850 --> 00:48:09.270
And they are a distributor
of many, many books

00:48:09.270 --> 00:48:10.680
and publications.

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:12.330
And what they do
is they're actually

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:17.160
able to use text-to-speech to
provide audio book previews

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:20.520
and readouts of all the
various books that they have.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:24.480
So just again, interesting use
cases for the speech-to-text

00:48:24.480 --> 00:48:26.910
API.

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:28.790
And with that, I
will call on Prabhu

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:32.850
to take us through
the takeaways.

00:48:32.850 --> 00:48:34.260
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Perfect.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:35.580
Thanks, Dan.

00:48:35.580 --> 00:48:38.188
So if you have any questions,
we are happy to answer.

00:48:38.188 --> 00:48:40.230
[? We ?] [? don't ?]
[? know if ?] Google Home is

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:40.860
here.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:41.640
So no issues.

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:53.320
To wrap up, first, from a
project, this is a one way--

00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:55.580
technology was one aspect of it.

00:48:55.580 --> 00:48:58.000
But really, understanding
the human interactions,

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:03.550
like how users will request for
a book, request for it to play,

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:04.930
request for it to search--

00:49:04.930 --> 00:49:08.350
having that interaction
clearly laid out.

00:49:08.350 --> 00:49:09.370
Then descend for scale.

00:49:09.370 --> 00:49:12.130
Like implementing
a project, like

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:14.110
to load 100 books
versus a million.

00:49:14.110 --> 00:49:17.710
We want to have the solution,
to have the scale and the reach.

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:19.780
Then, very important, security.

00:49:19.780 --> 00:49:23.080
These particular
resources are copyrighted.

00:49:23.080 --> 00:49:26.410
Not for everyone to log
in and then go through it.

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:28.000
And then integrations.

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:31.300
Today, we want to do for a
certain platform like, say,

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:33.910
Google Home, but we want
to make it agnostic.

00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:36.760
Like if users have
their forms that they

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:40.940
have Google Assistant with or
any other modes of interaction,

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:43.930
we want to have a platform that
supports multiple integrations

00:49:43.930 --> 00:49:44.770
into it.

00:49:44.770 --> 00:49:49.060
So key takeaways-wise to quickly
wrap up-- human interactions,

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:53.200
conversational style, design for
scale, security built into it,

00:49:53.200 --> 00:49:55.432
and support for
multiple integrations.

00:49:55.432 --> 00:49:56.140
Thanks, everyone.

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:59.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

